The period from 1606 to 1648 covers the four decades between the arrival of the Baiduist exiles and the outbreak of the [[Sinchew War]]. It is the period in which the colony's modern population was formed.

More than a million people were transported to Sinchew after 1606 under the [[Baiduist Expulsion from Zong|Baiduist expulsion]] ordered by the court of Zong, arriving without notice in a colony whose population in 1605 was 94,304. Famine followed in 1607, 1609 and 1611. The colony bought frontier land from [[Hachinawa]] to settle the arrivals and joined Hachinawa in the [[Third Mahik War]] of 1614 to 1618, which transferred the [[Timara]] country to the two of them. A religious society founded among the exiles in the 1620s became the army that began the war in 1648.

Three governors held office in the period: [[Tân Khéng-hui]] from 1606 to 1619, [[Lîm Bûn-giâm]] from 1619 to 1635, and [[Ng Tek-seng]] from 1635 to 1648. Each was nominated by the colony's assembly for appointment by the crown.

== Early Baiduist Expulsion (1606–1619) ==

[[Tân Khéng-hui]] (陳慶輝) was governor from 1606 to 1619.

The span covers the first phase of the [[Baiduist Expulsion from Zong|Baiduist expulsion]], in which the court of Zong removed the Baiduist population from the mainland and transported it to Sinchew. The transportation began in 1606 and continued throughout the period. More than a million people were landed in the colony during it, and the first famines followed in 1607, 1609 and 1611.

=== Destination of the exiles ===

The expulsion of the Baiduists from Zong was decided on religious grounds and applied to the whole kingdom. The question of where the expelled population should be sent was settled separately, and it was settled in favour of Sinchew rather than of [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]]. Four grounds for the choice of Sinchew are recorded.

The first concerned the colony's government. The governorship had come to be filled by nomination of the colony's assembly, which submitted a name for appointment by the crown. The practice was tolerated and had become established, but it made the governor the nominee of the governed, and it had arisen from a vacancy during the [[Great Peasant Revolt (Zong)|Great Peasant Revolt]] rather than from any grant.

The second concerned the colony's part in the disorders of the mainland. The plantation economies of Sinchew and Louyang had produced a quantity of colonial goods that exceeded what the Zong market could absorb, and the resulting fall in prices ruined producers in the northern provinces. The colonies profited from the shortage their own goods had produced, and the court attributed the disorders of 1572 to 1585 in part to them.

The third concerned the colony's conduct during the [[Baiduist usurpation]]. When [[Foyuan of Zong|Foyuan]] took the throne, the colonial government at Tamsui did not immediately refuse his authority. Other administrations did, and were able to plead their fidelity afterwards. Sinchew's delay was remembered.

The fourth concerned the [[Canglan Rebellion]] of about 1605, which had been financed in part by Baiduist merchant houses holding property in Sinchew.

The transportation served two purposes. It punished a colony suspected of independence and of disloyalty, and it removed from the mainland a population whose religion the court had resolved to extirpate. The expulsion and the choice of destination were ordered as a single measure and the record treats them as one.

=== Arrival ===

The first ships arrived without announcement. The colonial administration had no notice of the numbers to be landed, no provision for their reception, and no authority over the process by which they were carried.

The return of 1605 records a population of 94,304. More than a million people were landed over the following decades.

=== Famines ===

Famine followed in 1607, 1609 and 1611. The colony's agriculture could not feed the population landed on it, the arrivals had no land and no means of obtaining it, and the transport that carried them continued to be directed from Zong rather than from the colony.

The famines of these years were the first of a series. The pattern of the period was a population exceeding what the colony could provision, sustained by imports and by the opening of new land, and failing when either stopped.

=== Economy ===

The colony's agriculture before 1606 was organised for export. Sugarcane, tea and timber were grown for sale to Zong and, after the trade edict of 1577, to other Fosian and Illypnian markets, and the food the colony consumed was imported. Its plantations had been worked by enslaved labour brought from [[Dagit]] and the [[Balausia Peninsula]] under the [[Secret Trade]].

The arrival of the exiles changed the labour supply and the food balance at the same time. The colony now contained more than a million people who had been landed without purchase, and the cost of employing them was below the cost of procuring, carrying and maintaining a captive. The [[Secret Trade]] had been conducted unlawfully for seventy years and it did not survive the competition. Its last vessels arrived in the 1610s, and the enslaved population thereafter grew only by natural increase.

Cash crops. Sugarcane remained the principal export throughout the period. The plantation districts were held by the Tianist minority and by the merchant houses of the coast, and the cane land was not distributed to the arrivals. Tea, grown on higher ground, expanded as the Fosian and Illypnian markets took it, and timber continued to be cut from the interior.

Food. The colony imported grain. Its own agriculture was directed to crops that could be sold, and the population that arrived had to be fed from outside. Hachinawa was the principal source, and the sales of frontier land were accompanied by the sale of grain from the same empire. The dependence was complete enough that the administration's ability to feed Tamsui was a function of its relations with the Hachina court.

Subsistence. The Baiduists were settled on land unsuitable for the plantation economy, and they grew for their own consumption on it: tubers, vegetables and maize, with paddy where the ground permitted and the water could be controlled. This sector produced nothing for export and is not represented in the colony's trade returns, and by 1640 it supported the greater part of the population.

The consequence for the colony's accounts was a permanent imbalance between what it sold and what it bought. Its exports were plantation produce; its imports were grain. Between 1606 and 1648 the administration was never able to feed the population it had been given from the produce of the land it held.

=== Population and settlement ===

The exiles were Baiduist and the colony's administration was Tianist, because the crown appointed it and the crown had made Baiduism the object of the expulsion. The Tianist minority held the offices, the courts and the grants of land. The Baiduist majority held none of them, and was settled where circumstances permitted rather than where the administration would have chosen.

Settlement was concentrated on land the administration did not want, which was land unsuitable for the plantation economy. The largest settlement of this kind was along the shores of [[Tn̂g-ô͘]], the great northern lake, whose steep margins were fit for neither cash crops nor paddy.

Three means of managing the arrivals were available to the administration, and it used all three. It settled them where land was cheap and unclaimed. It taxed them for the support of an administration that employed none of them. And it required them to live outside [[Tamsui]], so that the capital remained a Tianist town.

=== Purchase of frontier land ===

Hachinawa had been selling frontier land since the 1580s, and its gold revenue had been failing since the end of the sixteenth century. The transfer of a million people to a colony on its northern frontier provided a buyer with an immediate need and the means to pay, and the sales of this period were larger than those of the preceding decades.

Hachinawa obtained three things from the sales. It sold land at prices set by its own need rather than the buyer's; it obtained a market for grain in a colony that could not feed itself; and it obtained the support of the colony in a military campaign.

=== Third Mahik War ===

{{Main|Third Mahik War}}

The area to the south and south-east of Hachinawa was held by [[Timara]] chiefdoms rather than by a single polity. The colony and the empire agreed in 1614 to divide it, and the war ended in their favour in 1618.

The settlement transferred the Timara country to the two victors and was concluded by the [[Treaty of Tamsui (1618)|Treaty of Tamsui]] of 1618, which fixed the frontier between the colony and the empire. Under the boundary established in that year, Hachinawa held about 1,581,000 km² and the colony about 600,000 km².

== Land Sales and Settlement (1619–1635) ==

[[Lîm Bûn-giâm]] (林文嚴) was governor from 1619 to 1635.

The span covers the second phase of the transportation, in which the colony continued to buy frontier land from Hachinawa and to settle the arrivals on it, and the Baiduist population organised itself into the bodies that governed its own affairs.

=== Purchase and settlement ===

The colony's need for land continued throughout the span and Hachinawa continued to sell it. The arrivals of the first phase were settled on land bought in this one, and the pattern established between 1606 and 1619 continued: settlement on ground the administration did not want, and the exclusion of the Baiduist majority from office and from grants.

=== Ocean Crossing Society ===

{{Main|Ocean Crossing Society}}

The army that began the Sinchew War was not formed for the purpose. It began as a religious and mutual-aid society and became an army over two decades.

The Ocean Crossing Society (海渡會, Hái-tō͘-hōe) was founded in the 1620s among the Baiduist settlements of the interior by Chiu Hoat-bêng (周法明), a monk of the exiles. Its objects were the preservation of Baiduist practice and the provision of mutual support among the arrivals. It maintained the observance of festivals and the ordination of monks, collected contributions for the relief of the destitute, and provided for burial and for the support of widows and orphans.

Two measures of the colonial administration contributed to its growth. The exiles were assessed for the support of an administration that excluded them from office and from land grants, and the assessment fell on a population that had arrived with nothing. The administration also required the Baiduists to live outside [[Tamsui]], and the settlements they established in the interior were outside the reach of the colony's ordinary government, where the society operated with less interference than the administration's officers could exert.

The society supplied services the administration did not provide: relief in the famines, arbitration between households, the education of children and the burial of the dead. By the 1630s it had branches in most of the Baiduist settlements, and its officers were the men to whom the community turned rather than the officials of the colony.

=== Baiduists in Hachinawa ===

Numbers of Baiduists left the colony for [[Hachinawa]], where Baiduist communities took up cultivation on land the empire had bought from the colony. They do not appear in the colony's returns and their number is not recorded in the colonial record. The Hachinawa account treats them as settlers within the empire. The free native population of the colony, which had fallen in every return between 1540 and 1605, increased from 1610 partly through the movement of Hahkina cultivators onto the same frontier land.

== Approach to the War (1635–1648) ==

[[Ng Tek-seng]] (黃德生) was governor from 1635 to 1648. He declined the proposal of the [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]] administration to invade [[Hachinawa]], and was governor when the [[Sinchew War]] began.

=== Louyang ===

The colony of [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]] had advanced northward along the [[Tsuwasha]] and by the 1610s had reached its upper course on Hachinawa's southern frontier.

Louyang petitioned the Sinchew administration in secret to join it in an invasion of Hachinawa, proposing to divide the empire between the two colonies. Louyang's interest was the territory between itself and the sea.

The administration at Tamsui declined. Its judgement was that Hachinawa would not be able to resist the military power of Zong, and that the invasion would therefore succeed, but that the colony's own population was too small to hold what it took. The proposal was not refused on principle and was not withdrawn.

Both colonies were possessions of Zong, and the rivalry was one between two colonial administrations of the same power. Their interests had diverged at the [[Tamsui Plot]] of 1543, when the territory was divided between them.

=== Outbreak of the Sinchew War ===

By the 1640s the population continued to arrive and to exceed what the colony could feed, the Baiduist majority remained excluded from land and office, and the settlement of the exiles along the margins of Tn̂g-ô͘ had produced a concentration of population in one region, on land nobody else wanted.

The [[Sinchew War]] began in 1648, and the revolutionary army was raised from the Ocean Crossing Society along the lake. In the opening years of the war the administration at Tamsui asked Hachinawa for assistance in a domestic matter, which was the first such request it had made.

== Demographics ==

The administration continued to take a return every five years and altered the classes it recorded. The four classes of the period before 1606 — free Zong, indentured Zong, native slaves and free natives — did not describe a colony in which the principal division was between the Tianist administration and the Baiduist exiles. The returns from 1610 record four classes: Tianist, Baiduist, enslaved, and free natives.

