The Tianist population increased from 47,818 to 87,592 over forty years. The Baiduist population, absent from the return of 1605, reached 1,306,748 by 1645, and the whole of the colony's growth in the period is attributable to it.

The free native population had fallen in every return between 1540 and 1605, from 4,197 to 1,032. From 1610 it increased, reaching 48,619 by 1645. The increase has two causes: natural growth, and the movement of Hahkina cultivators onto frontier land their own court had sold to the colony.

The enslaved population grew at about one per cent a year throughout, against three and four per cent in the preceding period. The trade that had supplied it did not survive the arrival of the exiles, and the enslaved population thereafter grew only by natural increase. Some vessels continued to arrive, and the returns to 1645 imply a small and diminishing traffic.

The famines of 1607, 1609 and 1611 appear in the returns as a rate rather than as an entry. The return of 1610 records 267,804 Baiduists.

